#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106771} :activity/id #uuid "0a79b6d7-a30d-5f46-addd-9ad83b0960c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105659 :question/id #uuid "9bdb609d-4000-5ee3-9267-71ed6a8dd140" :question/math-academy-id "e-7392"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435657}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"} :step/id #uuid "4e3d714f-5ee5-588e-a541-2b56e8ec5909" :step/math-academy-id 27400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105660 :question/id #uuid "cbc5bcb5-2c64-5895-ba1c-cc6d86207667" :question/math-academy-id "e-7393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435656}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "2e6311d2-17da-5bd4-a8bb-025d853635b4" :step/math-academy-id 27401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107105 :tutorial/content "A **binomial distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (or trials) where each outcome is either success or failure. In a binomial distribution, the probability of success in each experiment is the same.\n\nIf there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of successes has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nFor example, if we flip a coin $5$ times, then the number of heads follows a binomial distribution.\n\n- Each toss is an experiment that either results in success (i.e., heads) or failure (i.e., tails).\n\n- There are $n=5$ experiments.\n\n- Each experiment has a probability $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of success.\n\nSo, the number of heads has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution consisting of $n$ experiments (or trials) and the probability of success on each trial is $p,$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn words, we say, \"the random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution with $n$ trials and probability $p$ of success on each trial.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e7bad04-ccb9-574b-9792-eb4b8a51b62b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4451 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf257099-cebd-5c9d-9671-6b14b1f63d0d" :step/math-academy-id 27402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105661 :question/id #uuid "1fab59f6-f640-5533-9bff-4946dfd71f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-7394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411574}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "23b77bef-14a9-5128-9f05-05b523b37275" :step/math-academy-id 27403 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105664 :question/id #uuid "b4cfa71b-69f8-5cdb-b3c9-6dabdb32e264" :question/math-academy-id "e-7444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435655}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "27015fb3-f631-50ed-9981-c2dd89b9bb19" :step/math-academy-id 27600}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106014}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420379} :activity/id #uuid "8e2ae5f5-fad4-5930-b4b2-be1adc26af67" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d54eb6-7a30-5f6b-bd3e-dc209bc41ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420356} {:answer/id #uuid "7f818466-8ee3-5fe2-9b05-9ed2ed0063a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420359} {:answer/id #uuid "985603eb-a536-5f36-917b-88d373887aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420360} {:answer/id #uuid "a593bd02-ade6-58ad-8803-fcb266ea11b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420361} {:answer/id #uuid "dba0f373-c7ff-539e-91d7-ee5056b09dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420359} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d02abe-a662-5d74-8d74-1566af6e8ecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "790209a3-d18d-523b-a83b-f000e1032859" :question/math-academy-id "q-130421" :question/problem "Ann has a collection of $20$ pairs of shoes, $12$ of which are sports shoes. Every working day she randomly picks a pair of shoes to go to work, and she has to work for $26$ days next month. What is the probability that she will choose a pair of sports shoes exactly $10$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of times Ann will choose a pair of sports shoes in those $26$ days.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=26$ working days next month\n\n- since there are $12$ pairs of sports shoes out of $20$ pairs in the collection, the probability that she will pick a pair of sports shoes on a given day is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{12}{20}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(26,\\frac{3}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=10$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{26}{10}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{26-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{26!}{10!(26-10)!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{26!}{10!16!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{16} \\\\  & ≈0.014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "2105619e-8693-5c1a-b9c4-86f38be603c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420376}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19893c26-ed3a-5413-afc2-a0d8f99cb004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263243} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7845b2-eff1-53f6-ac81-13b44ac66e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263368} {:answer/id #uuid "30a8d306-0d26-54c5-9bcb-4815f9dcbe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.407" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263414} {:answer/id #uuid "9c845a96-9529-5da5-883a-d69c35a7093e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264340} {:answer/id #uuid "b8934eb6-83b1-5f57-b0a7-224ad71b202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.889" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263414} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac393bd-fb71-5e07-86e1-307bf6913530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d0bf184d-d7ca-56cb-ba56-14f65e4f1f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-130746" :question/problem "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $2:1.$ Four chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=4$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $2:1,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,\\frac{2}{3}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.407\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "29cfbfba-2513-567f-b12d-fc0e4b4fa288" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a3a7fc-b93c-517e-bc20-23ebc61bbf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.737" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420363} :answer-field/id #uuid "d29ba2bc-6b0d-55f1-9a0b-b4874a45e108" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2532926b-6b81-5b1e-a7b4-0b4cbbf77029" :question/math-academy-id "q-276040" :question/problem "An unfair coin has an $80%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that the coin landed on heads at least four times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=80%=0.8.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the coin landed on heads at least $4$ of the $5$ games. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.8)^{4}(1-0.8)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.8)^{5}(1-0.8)^{5-5} \\\\  & =5(0.8)^{4}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.737,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places"} :step/id #uuid "5b5b31f0-1b55-5e0a-8163-ed1fa2d9dca0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d415ee2-ce66-5299-b7c3-7e5330a401ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420355} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b37c8-0318-5da2-874d-1c31332f782d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e763d61-0831-5bdd-b6c6-c5e10a44b471" :question/math-academy-id "q-331639" :question/problem "A wildlife camera detects animals with a $40%$ success rate. The camera is triggered $7$ times during the night. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it detects an animal at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the camera successfully detected an animal.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ triggers.\n\n- The probability of detection is $p=40%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of detecting an animal at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=7)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{7-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{7}+7(0.4)(0.6)^{6}+21(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.580,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "63b8acd1-de5d-58b3-b19d-30965be2a286"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b8ddff-6335-5004-bb96-e61f512db03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420357} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2984c7-4c05-5944-a38d-1e1089d0fd33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420358} {:answer/id #uuid "b8af0a53-082c-5c70-9f61-4348a58b3949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420362} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd4b49b-3871-5ead-9a7d-e5c9ef7a0761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420365} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4df031-007e-5fce-a926-ac3f42667005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420357} :answer-field/id #uuid "304334c9-f645-591e-bc70-9dbb97961017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01dbc8f6-a3cd-5172-a886-7310e77fa973" :question/math-academy-id "q-84756" :question/problem "A student who answers randomly has a $50%$ chance of correctly answering each question on a $12$-question test. What is the probability that the student selects exactly $10$ correct answers? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers that the student gets.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ questions\n\n- the probability of hitting each question is $p=50%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.5).$ We want to find the probability of getting $x=10$ correct answers.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{10})(0.5)^{10}(1-0.5)^{12-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{10!(12-10)!}(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =66(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.016\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "7e04be3f-fd63-5a74-ad92-baa1c03bd6c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420375}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420354}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435659} :activity/id #uuid "72ce6bb1-8990-56c4-ba78-cb52ead69a49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e78e4d-d3e6-5a87-a64a-a52faa941ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435638} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeab778f-c7ec-5e9d-a3d3-497eb92a9d85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1fbc871-5104-5d0c-b476-cd8414e81086" :question/math-academy-id "q-276107" :question/problem "An unfair coin has a $15%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "07a22149-edba-534b-8341-9087e596b91c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ebbeff3-c5ba-5410-a408-4b1fcdd81dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435636} {:answer/id #uuid "318f6f4d-16fa-51e6-b179-78e91115f020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435637} {:answer/id #uuid "87d0f7be-dc1b-5137-82d6-ad06ca042afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.524" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435640} {:answer/id #uuid "90044cbf-45a0-57af-832e-49250df1a7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.555" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435642} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b00b74-2769-5031-9b6b-ad1e823bdbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.444" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435642} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c18810-179b-53f0-a245-19fdca7de9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1276b85-cb47-56f4-9380-ccdb52fdaba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89400" :question/problem "In a coffee shop, $28%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28%=0.28$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.28).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(1-0.28)}^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(0.72)}^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., the customer orders the coffee to go) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+12{(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+66{(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+220{(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & =0.555\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "4c7aaf17-b2f0-56e9-b409-e8d31a1d7256" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b365ee9-c153-5ddd-a40d-da2203634354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435641} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fee46f-9f97-52a3-886a-81612f3c88c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8e22e39-9704-57eb-a912-cb7e8741cbdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-331490" :question/problem "A fair coin is flipped $4$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ flips.\n\n- The probability of getting heads on a single flip is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =0.375,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "a30dec52-53b5-50a0-bd69-3bd1b30e2334" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca83564-ebe2-59c0-a75f-f0b7a4ff3ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.683" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435647} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40b2fb2-406e-5639-97e3-0ab4b3c53608" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "02d7c8e8-c1c1-5349-b786-2cc03d634147" :question/math-academy-id "q-331528" :question/problem "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "b3a85073-43c3-5155-b787-157bb738d375"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f0b3b1-6694-51fa-b8a9-943b0a524d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.871" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435635} {:answer/id #uuid "69c9acef-9d33-557c-bd83-45aa9da4aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.931" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435639} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b21599-df3a-56c7-be2f-08eed20c971f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.738" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435643} {:answer/id #uuid "c283cf27-9162-56a8-84b2-ce532ab362b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435644} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a7844-0f0f-53a0-969b-4a261303ee14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.973" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435639} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0f991d-9f23-5d9c-9970-8ed3243602c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4aa4655e-20ee-52b5-8d5f-be71eb721eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-89402" :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e43d6552-8e48-5f1e-9be0-84d2d3b26aca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435658}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435634}]}
